SUMMARY:Religion in Asia: Lab-Grown Bliss: The Prospects and Challenges of
  the Scientific Study of Asian Meditations — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261014T124500
DTEND;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261014T134500
DTSTAMP:20261011T191501Z
UID:7cb86a2278b44d73780514b60a6d@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Academic\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:Accounts of meditative attainments such as states of jhāna (a
 bsorption)\, nirodha (cessation of consciousness)\, or luminous pure aware
 ness are familiar from countless stories in Buddhist scriptures. If you ha
 d asked scholars a few decades ago\, they would have suggested folklore st
 udies as the appropriate discipline. Today\, as accounts of meditation pra
 ctice have become widely known\, these states are studied in neuroscientis
 ts’ labs. Just over a year ago\, Professor Eyal Aviv helped to organize 
 a conference that brought together neuroscientists and Buddhist Studies sc
 holars. Instead of the expected convergence of interests\, what emerged wa
 s a series of surprising disagreements. In this talk\, Pr\nCategory: Acade
 mic\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/religion-in-asia-lab
 -grown-bliss-the-prospects-and-challenges-of-the-scientific-study-of-asian
 -meditations
LOCATION:1957 E Street NW\, 1957 E St NW\, Washington DC 20052
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/religion-in-asia-lab-grown-bliss-the-pr
 ospects-and-challenges-of-the-scientific-study-of-asian-meditations
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:A Gift in Motion — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261014T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261014T170000
DTSTAMP:20261011T191501Z
UID:fcf66bab05a9055b4cdfe4aa1841@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:A multidisciplinary showcase from the artists and scholars of 
 the full-time faculty and staff at the Corcoran School of the Arts & Desig
 n. A Gift In Motion loosely draws from Robin Wall Kimmerer’s book The Se
 rviceberry which uplifts Indigenous frameworks of the natural world to adv
 ance the possibilities in collectivity\, reciprocity\, and the mindset of 
 abundance. These are anti-capitalist models and theories that provide insi
 ght into the systems of power we negotiate\, but also offer a lens on how 
 we individually move through our daily lives—together—through historic
 ally fraught landscapes. A keen reminder that working with\, for\, and bec
 ause of each other remains the most human motivation\, eve\nCategory: Arts
  & Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/a-gift-in-mot
 ion
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/a-gift-in-motion
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
